# vehje-runtime-zig. Design

The Zig implementation of the vehje runtime: the executor of a `Checked`
residual. It consumes the tier-tagged wire residual across the C ABI
(`vehje-runtime-abi`), evaluates it against the runtime environment, and
produces a value. It pulls no `std`, holds no allocator, and works over
host-lent memory.

It implements no build-environment semantics. Constant evaluation and macro
expansion are reductions the Rust compile side has already discharged, and the
effect proof guarantees no such construct survives into the residual, so the
runtime never has to recognise one.

## The tier-0 execution model

State: shipped. The `Arena` tier interprets the serialized node arena directly.
It is the reference semantics, the form the `Bytecode` tier (the CFG-of-blocks
linear form, intended) is later measured against for agreement and for speed.

The decode mirrors `vehje-runtime-abi`'s tier-0 serialization exactly: a
seven-word header, then fixed seven-word node records, then the flat
child-index pool, then the string blob, little-endian throughout so every
target reads the same bytes. Sections are found by arithmetic on the header's
counts, so reaching node `i`, a pool entry, or the blob needs no scan.

Binders match by their `Sym` bits. The wire carries one word per binder, the
stable binder identity the compile side minted, so a `Var` binds to its `Let`
by integer equality. The runtime holds no interner and never sees a name.

## Environments

State: shipped. An environment is a caller-lent bump arena of linked bindings,
not a popping stack. A binding records its binder's `Sym` bits, its value, and
the binding it extends; an environment is the index of a chain head.

The reason is closures. Capturing an environment costs recording one index, and
a closure that outlives the scope it was written in still reads the bindings it
captured. A popping stack cannot promise that, because the entries the closure
referred to would be gone or reused by the time it is called. Nothing is freed
within a run, and exhausting the lent arena is a named refusal, not an overrun.

## The evaluated set

State: shipped.

- Literals: unit, boolean, and integer.
- `Var`, resolved against the environment chain by `Sym` bits.
- `Let`, including the recursive form. A recursive binding is in scope for its
  own value, so the closure that value produces captures a chain already naming
  it, which is what lets a recursive function reach itself.
- `If`, evaluating its condition and then exactly one branch.
- `Lambda`, producing a closure over the environment it was evaluated in, so
  scoping is lexical: a closure reads the binding visible where it was written,
  not one that happens to shadow it at the call site.
- `Apply`, applying one argument at a time, because multi-parameter lambdas
  desugar to nested `Lambda`.

## The refusal set

State: shipped. Every failure is a named error, never a wrong answer: an
unbound variable, an application whose callee is not a closure, a form or
literal kind this tier does not evaluate, an exhausted environment arena, and a
corrupt index or record reaching past the image.

## The C ABI surface

State: shipped. `vehje_runtime_new`, `vehje_runtime_free`,
`vehje_runtime_execute`, and the `VEHJE_RESULT_OK`, `VEHJE_RESULT_ERR`,
`VEHJE_RESULT_NULL_HANDLE`, and `VEHJE_RESULT_INVALID_INPUT` constants
mirroring the Rust discriminants.

`vehje_runtime_execute` takes a sink pointer alongside the residual bytes. A
null sink means the host wanted only the outcome, so evaluating is the whole
job; a non-null one receives the produced value.

### `VehjeResult` ABI layout. scalar i32

The Rust side declares `#[repr(i32)] enum VehjeResult { Ok = 0, Err = -1,
NullHandle = -2, InvalidInput = -3 }`. On System V x86-64 and Apple ARM64 this
lowers to a scalar-in-register return. The Zig side returns plain `i32`
directly, giving identical register-use on every target.

Alternative considered and rejected: mirror the Rust enum via
`pub const VehjeResult = extern struct { code: i32 }` (a one-field wrapper
struct). Rejected because on MSVC Windows x64 a one-field extern struct returns
via hidden-pointer (sret) while a `#[repr(i32)]` enum returns in `eax`, so the
two sides would disagree on the calling convention. A scalar `i32` return is
the trivially portable form across every ABI.

Named constants mirror the Rust discriminants exactly and give Zig-side call
sites a readable form instead of bare integer literals.

## The produced value crossing back

State: shipped. The value is marshalled into the value-image layout pinned by
round `202607251109` and committed through the host's reserve-then-commit sink,
which lends the backing up front so the runtime holds no output memory after the
call. Writing the whole image in one reservation is the degenerate single-chunk
case the transfer contract allows.

A closure is refused with a named reason rather than partially written. It names
an environment inside this run, so it has no value-arena representation and
cannot be a value the host still holds after the call returns. A reservation the
host refuses is backpressure, not a crash.

## Intended, not shipped

- Family operations and host calls. The framework owns no family, so `Raw` is
  dispatched to a consumer-provided handler rather than interpreted here. This
  is how arithmetic and host services arrive, and it is the runtime half of the
  handler discipline.
- Strings, sequences, and records as values, and the remaining Core forms
  (`Project`, `Match`, `Iter`, `Interp`, `Handle`).
- The `Bytecode` tier over the CFG-of-blocks form.
- Per-session arena state, and diagnostics mapped to the ABI diagnostic shape.

## Non-scope

- The ABI contract itself (`vehje-runtime-abi`).
- The compiler-side dispatch and the embedding of this artifact
  (`vehje-runtime-driver`).
- Any build-environment semantics. The effect proof discharges them before the
  residual is emitted.

## Build integration

- `build.zig` drives the Zig compilation.
- Output: `zig-out/lib/libvehje_runtime.{so|dylib|dll}`.
- Target-triple-specific builds; a distribution bundle pairs the Rust compiler
  binary with the matching dylib for each supported platform.
- The `.zig` tree is not built by the Rust workspace tooling. It is validated by
  running `zig build` and `zig build test`.

## Dependencies

- The standard Zig library only. No third-party Zig packages, and no runtime
  allocator: memory is host-lent.

